schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Structured Outputs prevents an LLM from inventing an out-of-list category"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    OpenAI's Structured Outputs feature can constrain a model's response to a
    fixed JSON-schema enum, such that the model cannot return a value outside
    that set — as OpenAI puts it, the model will not "hallucinat[e] an
    invalid enum value." Claude and Gemini offer equivalent schema-constrained
    output modes.
  evidence: >-
    Cited in the article from the OpenAI Structured Outputs guide
    (developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/structured-outputs), referenced in
    the article's own References section.
  interpretation: >-
    Used in the article as the mechanical fix for taxonomy sprawl: hand the
    model a fixed list of approved types/tags via a schema enum so it must
    choose from them rather than inventing new categories on every pass. The
    article treats this as the line between "AI that maintains your taxonomy"
    and "AI that replaces it nightly."
  uncertainty: >-
    Reproduces OpenAI's own claim about its feature's guarantee; not
    independently verified against OpenAI's API behavior for this ingestion.
    The claim about "Claude and Gemini offer the same" is the article's own
    characterization, not itself sourced to a specific Anthropic or Google
    document.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/ai-assisted-classification.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-e50c8f7e3b5d
  source_lineage: ai-assisted-classification.md
